docs/submission/DEMO.md:9:Timing, measured 13 September 2026 on the current CPU-only deployment (two smoke runs through the SSH tunnel): one full analyze-interval round trip takes about **24 seconds**. For a live run, trigger **Analyze interval** while narrating the publisher-marker segment so the answer is ready by the analysis beat, or pre-run one analysis before starting the clock and run the second one live. Only one query is served at a time; do not queue clicks.
docs/submission/DEMO.md:15:| 0:00–0:15 | Show seven synchronized torque traces | “After a contact incident, an engineer needs a defensible account of what changed. The manual way is scrolling seven synchronized 1 kHz traces and assembling notes by hand. Trace turns a recorded interval into measurements, a model interpretation, and evidence they can share.” |
docs/submission/DEMO.md:16:| 0:15–0:30 | Open Event 01 marker details | “This mark comes from the dataset publisher. It is an annotation, not a verified cause or model detection.” |
docs/submission/DEMO.md:18:| 0:55–1:10 | Click the input evidence link | “The answer links back to its exact input interval. Joint 2 has the largest measured range here; the model names J4 as its strongest disturbance. These are different quantities, and neither identifies the physical impact location.” |
docs/submission/DEMO.md:24:Do not memorize a generated answer as a guaranteed future result. Read the actual response. The model and measured quantities can disagree; that is a reason to inspect evidence. The exact archived live response is in `demo/smoke-result.json`.
docs/submission/DEMO.md:28:**Why use a TSLM when the feature baseline wins?** The experiment establishes that direct feature methods are very competitive on this controlled dataset, whose labels are themselves derived from signal formulas. The decomposed confusion matrices show the trained OpenTSLM checkpoint matches the baseline's accuracy on the windows it answers (98.5% on 399 of 512); its measured gap is failing to produce a valid structured "no event" summary on 46.5% of free-motion windows. That is an output-reliability problem, not evidence the model misreads contact events — and it is the checkpoint's documented next improvement, to be selected on validation. The temporal-language interface additionally provides readable interpretations and free-form questions that a feature classifier cannot. We do not claim the TSLM is necessary for calculating peaks or ranges.
docs/submission/DEMO.md:30:**How is this faster than just looking at charts?** The manual workflow is scrolling seven synchronized 1 kHz traces and writing notes by hand; in Trace the same incident is marker → analyze → linked evidence → export in a few interactions, as shown in the demo. We deliberately state this as a workflow demonstration, not a measured time saving: the timed engineer study is prepared (`PILOT.md`) but has not been run.
docs/submission/DEMO.md:34:**What business value is demonstrated?** A traceable investigation and export workflow is demonstrated. Reduced debugging time is a hypothesis with a defined pilot; no customer ROI or downtime reduction has been measured.
docs/submission/DEMO.md:36:**Can it find an unknown event anywhere in a run?** The demo opens publisher-marked intervals. General unannotated incident localization and multi-recording raw-window inference are not demonstrated by the bundled fixture.
